---
description: Interpret causal callbacks and preserve their dramatic function.
args:
  intake: IntakeResult
  world: WorldIndex
  knowledge: KnowledgeIndex
returns: MotifIndex
---
1. Inventory explicit director callback notes and selected later echoes. Read their actual dramatic
   relation: recognition, trust, repetition, reversal or a changed interpretation of an earlier action.
2. Compare each later line to plausible earlier scene meanings. Similar words alone do not prove a
   motif link; a source-backed dramatic function and compatible actor perspective are required.
3. For an accepted link, record earlierSceneId, laterSceneId, meaning and requiredFactIds. Include
   the earlier meaning and the later echo as separate exact witnesses.
4. A callback saying 'you carried it to me' can rely on physical delivery, while a revision about
   carrying a message can preserve trust through oral communication. Keep that distinction explicit.
5. Consult knowledge to check the later speaker could recognize the shared experience. An audience
   echo can be a motif without becoming a character's asserted knowledge of an unseen event.
6. Keep unlicensedEchoes where a later selected line depends on an absent or contradicted event.
   Keep unresolved where two possible interpretations are both supported and no directive chooses one.
7. Preserve director-protected ambiguity in a motif's function. A reconciliation need not resolve
   whether a withheld letter reflected protection or self-interest.
8. Return links, unlicensedEchoes and unresolved. This branch owns dramatic dependency meaning;
   it does not add physical custody or narrator truth to make an aesthetically pleasing link work.
